# Authentication Boundary Contract

## CSRF and browser binding

- Use the Rails forgery-protection implementation for state-changing browser requests. Do not add
  custom acceptance in `verified_request?`, `valid_request_origin?`, a controller override, a null
  session, or a skipped callback.
- A browser-bound admission may add a nonce or digest as defense in depth, but it never replaces
  Rails CSRF, Origin and Fetch Metadata checks. The nonce is server-issued, bound to the initiating
  browser and operation, single-use, and refused when stale, cross-browser, mismatched or replayed.
- GET and HEAD do not create reservations, candidates, proofs, grants or ceremony state. The only
  exception is the Sign-in path continuation GETs under the six conditions of
  `adr/sign-in-path-continuation-protocol.md`: Fetch Metadata navigation/document, host-only
  browser binding before touching the one-time record, atomic CAS/row-lock consumption, the
  same-browser issuance-plus-10-second repeat rule, `no-store`/`no-referrer`, and a clean final URL.
  HEAD is always refused there.

## Auth and step-up ownership

- Auth is a ceremony/workflow boundary. Do not add a general Auth login session, shared-domain
  authentication cookie, RP credential fallback, or authority token issuer. The D-82 Base/Auth
  admission binding is the only accepted exception.
- A step-up requirement is an explicit object. Preserve its purpose, allowed methods, freshness,
  user-verification, phishing-resistance, full-reauthentication, actor, resource, tenant, session
  and transaction bindings through admission, result delivery and final mutation.
- AAL labels are historical evidence labels only. They must not authorize an operation, substitute
  for a missing requirement, or be translated into a stronger requirement.

Unexpected, missing or contradictory evidence is an explicit refusal. Do not infer a substitute
credential, session, browser, actor or assurance level.
